<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning </h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tianyi Ma
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Statistical Laboratory, University of Cambridge
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tengyao Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Statistics, London School of Economics and Political Science
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Richard J. Samworth
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Statistical Laboratory, University of Cambridge
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study in-context learning problems where a Transformer is pretrained on tasks drawn from a mixture distribution <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\lambda_{\alpha}\pi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\lambda_{\alpha}\pi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, called the pretraining prior, in which each mixture component <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a distribution on tasks of a specific difficulty level indexed by <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to understand the performance of the pretrained Transformer when evaluated on a different test distribution <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of tasks of fixed difficulty <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, and with potential distribution shift relative to <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to the chi-squared divergence <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi_{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi_{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> being at most <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we consider nonparametric regression problems with random smoothness, and multi-index models with random smoothness as well as random effective dimension. We prove that a large Transformer pretrained on sufficient data achieves the optimal rate of convergence corresponding to the difficulty level <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, uniformly over test distributions <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> in the chi-squared divergence ball. Thus, the pretrained Transformer is able to achieve faster rates of convergence on easier tasks and is robust to distribution shift at test time. Finally, we prove that even if an estimator had access to the test distribution <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, the convergence rate of its expected risk over <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> could not be faster than that of our pretrained Transformers, thereby providing a more appropriate optimality guarantee than minimax lower bounds.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite> have emerged as one of the dominant architectures in modern machine learning, as they have achieved state-of-the-art performance in many domains such as natural language processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and protein prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jumper et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
A striking ability of pretrained Transformers, first observed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2020)</a></cite> in Large Language Models (LLMs), is the phenomenon of <em id="S1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">in-context learning</em> (ICL): given a prompt containing examples and a query, Transformers can learn the underlying pattern from the examples and produce accurate output for the query, without updating its parameters.
For instance, given the values of the function <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\mapsto xy^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\mapsto xy^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or ‘<math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{subfield}\mapsto\text{field}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>subfield</mtext><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mtext>field</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{subfield}\mapsto\text{field}</annotation></semantics></math>’ at a few distinct points, LLMs are able to recover the value of the function at a new point:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S1.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overbrace{\underbrace{f(1,1)=1,\;f(1,2)=4,\;f(2,2)=8,\;f(2,3)=18,}_{\text{examples}}\;\underbrace{f(3,3)=}_{\text{query}}}^{\text{prompt}}\underbrace{27\vphantom{f(2,2)}}_{\text{output}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover><mover accent="true"><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>examples</mtext></munder><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>query</mtext></munder></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏞</mo></mover><mtext>prompt</mtext></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mn>27</mn><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>output</mtext></munder></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overbrace{\underbrace{f(1,1)=1,\;f(1,2)=4,\;f(2,2)=8,\;f(2,3)=18,}_{\text{examples}}\;\underbrace{f(3,3)=}_{\text{query}}}^{\text{prompt}}\underbrace{27\vphantom{f(2,2)}}_{\text{output}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overbrace{\underbrace{\text{Genetics}\to\text{Biology},\;\text{Relativity}\to\text{Physics},}_{\text{examples}}\;\underbrace{\text{Topology}\to}_{\text{query}}}^{\text{prompt}}\underbrace{\text{Mathematics}\vphantom{y}}_{\text{output}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover><mover accent="true"><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mtext>Genetics</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>Biology</mtext><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mtext>Relativity</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>Physics</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>examples</mtext></munder><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mtext>Topology</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>query</mtext></munder></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏞</mo></mover><mtext>prompt</mtext></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>Mathematics</mtext><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>output</mtext></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overbrace{\underbrace{\text{Genetics}\to\text{Biology},\;\text{Relativity}\to\text{Physics},}_{\text{examples}}\;\underbrace{\text{Topology}\to}_{\text{query}}}^{\text{prompt}}\underbrace{\text{Mathematics}\vphantom{y}}_{\text{output}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The ICL ability of Transformers was also exploited by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hollmann et al. (2025)</a></cite> to build tabular foundation models, which they show to outperform many classical machine learning methods, such as tree-based and boosting algorithms, on regression and classification problems for tabular data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive studies on ICL have been conducted from various perspectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim, Nakamaki and Suzuki, 2024</a>, e.g.)</cite>; we refer the readers to Section <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1 Related work ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> for a more detailed literature review. However, most theoretical analyses of ICL have focused on the setting where the distribution of pretraining data (which we call the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pretraining prior</em>) and the distribution of the test data (which we call the <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">test distribution</em>) are the same. In practice, Transformers are pretrained on a variety of tasks of different difficulties, and the test distribution will typically exhibit <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distribution shift</em> from the pretraining prior. For example, we may train a Transformer on translation tasks between different languages, where Dutch–English translation is typically easier than Chinese–English, since Dutch and English are both West Germanic languages with shared structure. At test time, the Transformer is required to translate between two specific languages, while some linguistic patterns may appear more frequently in the test data than in the pretraining data. To formulate a theoretical framework, we focus on regression problems (in line with most prior work) and consider a Transformer pretrained on a mixture distribution containing tasks of different difficulties, but where the test distribution consists of tasks of a specific difficulty with potential distribution shift from the corresponding mixture component of the pretraining prior. We study the following natural but important questions:</p>
<blockquote id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Do pretrained Transformers adapt to the difficulty of the test distribution, and are they robust to distribution shift?</em></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Our contributions</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Proposition <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we provide a key test-time risk upper bound, which decomposes into a term that captures both the degree of distribution shift and the proximity of the pretrained Transformer to the posterior regression function, and another term that represents the rate of convergence of the posterior regression function to the true regression function.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Universal approximation and learnability ‣ 3 Transformers, universal approximation and learnability ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we prove a universal approximation theorem for Transformers with softmax attention and non-polynomial activation in FFN layers. We further show that a large Transformer pretrained on sufficient data can learn the posterior regression function arbitrarily well when the activation in FFN layers is one of <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{ReLU}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖫𝖴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{ReLU}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GELU}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖦𝖤𝖫𝖴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GELU}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{SiLU}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖲𝗂𝖫𝖴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{SiLU}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Sections <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 In-context learning for multi-index models ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we consider general nonparametric regression problems and multi-index models respectively, with regression functions belonging to Besov smoothness classes. We prove that the posterior regression function is optimally adaptive to the difficulty of every task in the support of the pretraining prior (and hence robust to distribution shift). In combination with the results mentioned above, this establishes the adaptivity and distributional robustness of pretrained Transformers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Lower bounds on the ICL excess risk ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we show that the expected risk of any estimator over the test distribution <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> cannot achieve a faster rate of convergence than our pretrained Transformers (even if the estimator is allowed to depend on <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>). This provides a more appropriate form of optimality guarantee for pretrained Transformers than that yielded by minimax lower bounds usually considered in ICL literature.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Related work</h3>

<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">In-context learning</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a large body of theoretical research that aims to explain the ICL ability of Transformers.
One line of work shows that Transformers can perform ICL by approximating certain algorithms, such as gradient descent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, functional gradient descent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng, Chen and Sra, 2024</a>)</cite>, basis function regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim, Nakamaki and Suzuki, 2024</a>)</cite>, reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin, Bai and Mei, 2024</a>)</cite> and domain adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hataya, Matsui and Imaizumi, 2024</a>)</cite>. Other lines of work analyse ICL through the lens of Bayesian inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nagler, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deora et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, meta-learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeon et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu, Wang and Yao, 2025</a>)</cite> and training dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang, Frei and Bartlett, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumano, Kera and Yamasaki, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuwataka and Suzuki, 2025</a>)</cite>. In particular, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2023)</a></cite> show that Transformers can perform gradient descent on penalised linear regression, generalised linear models and two-layer neural networks, as well as implement in-context algorithm selection, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim, Nakamaki and Suzuki (2024)</a></cite> prove that Transformers are minimax optimal for nonparametric regression. Both papers consider the case where the test distribution coincides with the pretraining prior. On the other hand, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2022)</a></cite> study the setting where the test prompts have different formats compared to the pretraining data, and provide consistency results under a Bayesian hidden Markov model. There are also numerous empirical studies on the behaviour and properties of ICL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirsch et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panwar, Ahuja and Goyal, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reuter et al., 2025</a>, e.g.)</cite>. Finally, we note the concurrent and independent work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wakayama and Suzuki (2025)</a></cite>, who study the adaptivity of ICL from a Bayesian perspective. Their results are complementary to ours, and neither subsumes the other.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Posterior contraction theory</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Posterior contraction theory is an active area of research in statistics that studies the concentration of the posterior distribution around a true data generating distribution (i.e. from a frequentist perspective). Pioneered by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosal, Ghosh and van der Vaart (2000)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and Wasserman (2001)</a></cite>, posterior contraction for Bayesian nonparametric models has been investigated in different problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosal, Lember and van der Vaart, 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">van der Vaart and van Zanten, 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Finocchio and Schmidt-Hieber, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Egels and Castillo, 2025</a>, e.g.)</cite>. In this paper, we modify the tools developed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosal, Lember and van der Vaart (2008)</a></cite> to derive rates of convergence for the posterior regression function. Interested readers are referred to the book by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosal and van der Vaart (2017)</a></cite> for an introduction to the field.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Notation</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]\coloneqq\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]\coloneqq\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[m:n]\coloneqq\{m,m+1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[m:n]\coloneqq\{m,m+1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and similarly <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[n:\infty)\coloneqq\{n,n+1,\ldots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n:\infty)\coloneqq\{n,n+1,\ldots\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\wedge b\coloneqq\min\{a,b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\wedge b\coloneqq\min\{a,b\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\vee b\coloneqq\max\{a,b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\vee b\coloneqq\max\{a,b\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{0}_{d}\coloneqq(0,\ldots,0)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{0}_{d}\coloneqq(0,\ldots,0)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{1}_{d}\coloneqq(1,\ldots,1)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{1}_{d}\coloneqq(1,\ldots,1)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the clipping operator <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{clip}_{R}:\mathbb{R}\to[-R,R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖼𝗅𝗂𝗉</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{clip}_{R}:\mathbb{R}\to[-R,R]</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{clip}_{R}(x)\coloneqq-R\vee x\wedge R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖼𝗅𝗂𝗉</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{clip}_{R}(x)\coloneqq-R\vee x\wedge R</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a Borel measurable set <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Vol}_{d}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Vol</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Vol}_{d}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Lebesgue measure of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
For two <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-finite measures <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> on a measurable space <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{X},\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{X},\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is absolutely continuous with respect to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, written as <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\ll Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\ll Q</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(A)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(A)=0</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is such that <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(A)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(A)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and in that case write <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}P}{\mathrm{d}Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}P}{\mathrm{d}Q}</annotation></semantics></math> for the Radon–Nikodym derivative. When <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> are probability measures, the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence from <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\chi^{2}(P,Q)\coloneqq\begin{cases}\int_{\mathcal{X}}\bigl(\frac{\mathrm{d}P}{\mathrm{d}Q}\bigr)^{2}\,\mathrm{d}Q-1\quad&amp;\text{if }P\ll Q\\
\infty&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\chi^{2}(P,Q)\coloneqq\begin{cases}\int_{\mathcal{X}}\bigl(\frac{\mathrm{d}P}{\mathrm{d}Q}\bigr)^{2}\,\mathrm{d}Q-1\quad&amp;\text{if }P\ll Q\\
\infty&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Further, we write <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold product measure of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{supp}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{supp}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> for the support of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the intersection of all closed <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(A)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(A)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. For a set <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of functions from <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and a set <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of functions from <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m50" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}\circ\mathcal{G}\coloneqq\{f\circ g:f\in\mathcal{F},\,g\in\mathcal{G}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\circ\mathcal{G}\coloneqq\{f\circ g:f\in\mathcal{F},\,g\in\mathcal{G}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\circ g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\circ g</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the composition of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. For a function <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f\|_{\infty}\coloneqq\sup_{x\in\mathcal{X}}|f(x)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f\|_{\infty}\coloneqq\sup_{x\in\mathcal{X}}|f(x)|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first present the generating mechanism of our pretraining data. Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}\subseteq\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}\subseteq\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be Borel measurable, and let <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> be a measurable space of probability distributions on <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, dominated by a common <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-finite measure. The <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pretraining prior</em> <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is a distribution on <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, and our pretraining data take the form <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)},X^{(t)},Y^{(t)})_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)},X^{(t)},Y^{(t)})_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, generated as follows:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Draw random distributions <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\ldots,P_{T}\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">iid</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\ldots,P_{T}\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, draw <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)}\coloneqq(X_{i}^{(t)},Y_{i}^{(t)})_{i=1}^{n}\sim P_{t}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≔</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)}\coloneqq(X_{i}^{(t)},Y_{i}^{(t)})_{i=1}^{n}\sim P_{t}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{(t)},Y^{(t)})\sim P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{(t)},Y^{(t)})\sim P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> independently.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, for each <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> examples, <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is our query and our goal is to predict its corresponding output <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>; see (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). We emphasise that the <em id="S2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">test distribution</em> <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> of new prompts at test time will typically be different from the (possibly unknown) distribution <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> that generated our pretraining data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, for a class <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of measurable functions from <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. Transformers), define the <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">empirical risk minimiser</em> <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{f}_{T}\in\argmin_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\widehat{\mathcal{R}}_{T}(f),\quad\text{where }\widehat{\mathcal{R}}_{T}(f)\coloneqq\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\bigl\{Y^{(t)}-f(X^{(t)},\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)})\bigr\}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">argmin</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{f}_{T}\in\argmin_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\widehat{\mathcal{R}}_{T}(f),\quad\text{where }\widehat{\mathcal{R}}_{T}(f)\coloneqq\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\bigl\{Y^{(t)}-f(X^{(t)},\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)})\bigr\}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.Ex2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">and <math id="S2.Ex2.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)},X^{(t)},Y^{(t)})_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)},X^{(t)},Y^{(t)})_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are generated according to <math id="S2.Ex2.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We remark that, given a new set of examples <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}\in(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}\in(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the prediction at a query <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}_{T}(x,D_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}_{T}(x,D_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and we do not need to update <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> accordingly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a distribution <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>-risk</em> of a measurable <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathcal{X}\times(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}\to\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathcal{X}\times(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}\to\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> as<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          We adopt the convention that
for <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> means that the random variables inside the expectation are generated from <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)\sim P^{\otimes(n+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)\sim P^{\otimes(n+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas for a distribution <math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="footnote1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="footnote1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\nu}\mathbb{E}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\nu}\mathbb{E}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> means that the distribution <math id="footnote1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is also random and drawn from <math id="footnote1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{\nu}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{\nu}(g)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{P\sim\nu}\mathbb{E}_{(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)|P\sim P^{\otimes(n+1)}}\bigl[\bigl\{Y-g(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]\equiv\mathbb{E}_{\nu}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{Y-g(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{P\sim\nu}\mathbb{E}_{(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)|P\sim P^{\otimes(n+1)}}\bigl[\bigl\{Y-g(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]\equiv\mathbb{E}_{\nu}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{Y-g(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})\bigr\}^{2}\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is random and depends on the pretraining data <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)},X^{(t)},Y^{(t)})_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{D}_{n}^{(t)},X^{(t)},Y^{(t)})_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we interpret <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\nu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\nu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> as the expectation conditional on the pretraining data, which is assumed to be independent of the test data <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\nu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\nu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> is also random. For a test distribution <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, we further define the <em id="S2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ICL excess <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-risk</em> of <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}^{\mathrm{ICL}}_{\mu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>ICL</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}^{\mathrm{ICL}}_{\mu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq\mathbb{E}\mathcal{R}_{\mu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})-\mathbb{E}_{\mu}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{Y-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq\mathbb{E}\mathcal{R}_{\mu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})-\mathbb{E}_{\mu}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{Y-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip 2.56064pt=\mathbb{E}\,\mathbb{E}_{\mu}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{\widetilde{f}_{T}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.538em">=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip 2.56064pt=\mathbb{E}\,\mathbb{E}_{\mu}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{\widetilde{f}_{T}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where the term <math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\mu}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{Y-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\mu}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{Y-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math> is the oracle risk assuming knowledge of the distribution <math id="S2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> that generates <math id="S2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and in particular of the true regression function <math id="S2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, and the equality is shown in the proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Finally, we define the <em id="S2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">posterior regression function</em> <math id="S2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\pi}:\mathcal{X}\times(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}\to\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\pi}:\mathcal{X}\times(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}\to\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> (with respect to <math id="S2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>) by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g_{\pi}(x,D_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g_{\pi}(x,D_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl(Y\,|\,X=x,\,\mathcal{D}_{n}=D_{n}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl(Y\,|\,X=x,\,\mathcal{D}_{n}=D_{n}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}\in(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}\in(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S2.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\pi}(x,D_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\pi}(x,D_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the posterior mean of <math id="S2.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S2.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{n}=D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{n}=D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)\,|\,P\sim P^{\otimes(n+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)\,|\,P\sim P^{\otimes(n+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\sim\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\sim\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition provides a key upper bound on the ICL excess <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-risk, enabling us to study the adaptivity and distributional robustness of ICL.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose that for all <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)|\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)|\leq R</annotation></semantics></math> almost surely.
Let <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be defined as in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), let <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}\coloneqq\mathsf{clip}_{R}\circ\widehat{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖼𝗅𝗂𝗉</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}\coloneqq\mathsf{clip}_{R}\circ\widehat{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(\widetilde{f}_{T})\coloneqq\mathbb{E}\mathcal{R}_{\pi}(\widetilde{f}_{T})-\mathcal{R}_{\pi}(g_{\pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(\widetilde{f}_{T})\coloneqq\mathbb{E}\mathcal{R}_{\pi}(\widetilde{f}_{T})-\mathcal{R}_{\pi}(g_{\pi})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}^{\mathrm{ICL}}_{\mu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})\leq 4R\sqrt{\bigl\{\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi)+1\bigr\}\mathcal{E}(\widetilde{f}_{T})}+2\mathbb{E}_{\mu}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>ICL</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}^{\mathrm{ICL}}_{\mu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})\leq 4R\sqrt{\bigl\{\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi)+1\bigr\}\mathcal{E}(\widetilde{f}_{T})}+2\mathbb{E}_{\mu}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The upper bound in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) consists of two terms. The first involves both the extent of the distribution shift measured by the <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence, as well as the expected difference between the <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-risk of <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and that of the posterior regression function <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well-known that <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> minimises the <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-risk <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> over all measurable functions, so intuitively, if <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a large Transformer pretrained on a sufficient corpus of data, then <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(\widetilde{f}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(\widetilde{f}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> should be negligible. Indeed, this is proved in our Proposition <a href="#Thmthm3" title="Proposition 3. ‣ 3.2 Universal approximation and learnability ‣ 3 Transformers, universal approximation and learnability ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The second term reflects the proximity of the posterior regression function to the true regression function. In fact, in Sections <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 In-context learning for multi-index models ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we will control <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly for all <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in the support of <math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. This reveals the interesting feature that only the first term on the right-hand side of (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) depends on the degree of distribution shift, and this term can be made arbitrarily small by increasing the size of the Transformer and the pretraining dataset, so that the second term ultimately determines the rate of convergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Transformers, universal approximation and learnability</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Transformers</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we formally define the classes of (encoder-only) Transformers that we will consider.
For a vector <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(v_{1},\ldots,v_{d})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(v_{1},\ldots,v_{d})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax(v)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖲𝗈𝖿𝗍𝗆𝖺𝗑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax(v)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the vector whose <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th entry is given by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(v_{j})/\sum_{\ell=1}^{d}\exp(v_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(v_{j})/\sum_{\ell=1}^{d}\exp(v_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>. Often, we will apply the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖲𝗈𝖿𝗍𝗆𝖺𝗑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> function to matrices, by which we mean that the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖲𝗈𝖿𝗍𝗆𝖺𝗑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> function is applied row-wise. Given <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}\geq d+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}\geq d+2</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">model dimension</em>, examples <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}=(x_{i},y_{i})_{i=1}^{n}\in(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}=(x_{i},y_{i})_{i=1}^{n}\in(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and a query <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the input matrix is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{\mathrm{in}}\equiv Z_{\mathrm{in}}(D_{n},x)\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}x_{1}^{\top}&amp;y_{1}&amp;\bm{0}_{d_{\mathrm{model}}-d-2}^{\top}&amp;0\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots\\
x_{n}^{\top}&amp;y_{n}&amp;\bm{0}_{d_{\mathrm{model}}-d-2}^{\top}&amp;0\\
x^{\top}&amp;0&amp;\bm{0}_{d_{\mathrm{model}}-d-2}^{\top}&amp;1\end{pmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{\mathrm{in}}\equiv Z_{\mathrm{in}}(D_{n},x)\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}x_{1}^{\top}&amp;y_{1}&amp;\bm{0}_{d_{\mathrm{model}}-d-2}^{\top}&amp;0\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots\\
x_{n}^{\top}&amp;y_{n}&amp;\bm{0}_{d_{\mathrm{model}}-d-2}^{\top}&amp;0\\
x^{\top}&amp;0&amp;\bm{0}_{d_{\mathrm{model}}-d-2}^{\top}&amp;1\end{pmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the last column of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathrm{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathrm{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> is our positional encoding used to identify the query, and we pad each row with zeros to make them <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional. A Transformer iteratively applies attention layers and feed forward network (FFN) layers to the input matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathrm{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathrm{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Letting <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denote an upper bound on the context length, our universal approximation theory (Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3.2 Universal approximation and learnability ‣ 3 Transformers, universal approximation and learnability ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) holds uniformly for all <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>, so it is convenient to consider Transformers as functions from<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            The space <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with the disjoint union topology and its corresponding Borel <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra.</span></span></span> <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now define attention layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefn1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Attention layer)<span id="Thmdefn1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in[H]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="Thmdefn1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">attention layer</em> with <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> heads and parameters <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}\coloneqq(Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h})_{h=1}^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}\coloneqq(Q_{h},K_{h},V_{h})_{h=1}^{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is the function <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Attn}_{\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}}:\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}\to\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝗇</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Attn}_{\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}}:\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}\to\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\attn_{\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝗇</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\attn_{\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq Z+\sum_{h=1}^{H}\softmax\biggl(\frac{ZQ_{h}K_{h}^{\top}Z^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{model}}}}\biggr)ZV_{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖲𝗈𝖿𝗍𝗆𝖺𝗑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ZQ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ZV</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq Z+\sum_{h=1}^{H}\softmax\biggl(\frac{ZQ_{h}K_{h}^{\top}Z^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{model}}}}\biggr)ZV_{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefn2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(FFN layer)<span id="Thmdefn2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}\times d_{\mathrm{ffn}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}\times d_{\mathrm{ffn}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{ffn}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{ffn}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="Thmdefn2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">FFN layer</em> with parameters <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}\coloneqq(W_{1},W_{2},v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}\coloneqq(W_{1},W_{2},v)</annotation></semantics></math> (and activation function <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>) is the function <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{FFN}_{\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}}:\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}\to\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖥𝖥𝖭</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{FFN}_{\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}}:\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}\to\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{FFN}_{\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}}(Z)\coloneqq Z+\rho(ZW_{1}+\bm{1}_{n+1}v^{\top})W_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖥𝖥𝖭</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{FFN}_{\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}}(Z)\coloneqq Z+\rho(ZW_{1}+\bm{1}_{n+1}v^{\top})W_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefn2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefn2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is applied entrywise.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now, a Transformer block is a composition of an attention layer and an FFN layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefn3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Transformer block)<span id="Thmdefn3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}</annotation></semantics></math> be as in Definitions <a href="#Thmdefn1" title="Definition 1 (Attention layer). ‣ 3.1 Transformers ‣ 3 Transformers, universal approximation and learnability ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmdefn2" title="Definition 2 (FFN layer). ‣ 3.1 Transformers ‣ 3 Transformers, universal approximation and learnability ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> respectively. The <em id="Thmdefn3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Transformer block</em> with parameters <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\theta_{\mathrm{attn}},\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\theta_{\mathrm{attn}},\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the function <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{TFBlock}_{(\theta_{\mathrm{attn}},\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}})}:\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}\to\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖳𝖥𝖡𝗅𝗈𝖼𝗄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{TFBlock}_{(\theta_{\mathrm{attn}},\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}})}:\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}\to\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{TFBlock}_{(\theta_{\mathrm{attn}},\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}})}\coloneqq\mathsf{FFN}_{\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}}\circ\attn_{\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖳𝖥𝖡𝗅𝗈𝖼𝗄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖥𝖥𝖭</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝗇</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{TFBlock}_{(\theta_{\mathrm{attn}},\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}})}\coloneqq\mathsf{FFN}_{\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}}\circ\attn_{\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefn3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We say that the Transformer block <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{TFBlock}_{(\theta_{\mathrm{attn}},\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖳𝖥𝖡𝗅𝗈𝖼𝗄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>attn</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{TFBlock}_{(\theta_{\mathrm{attn}},\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}})}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> heads, model dimension <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}</annotation></semantics></math> and FFN width <math id="Thmdefn3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ffn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ffn}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, a Transformer is a composition of Transformer blocks.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefn4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Transformers)<span id="Thmdefn4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(L,H,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>TF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(L,H,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})</annotation></semantics></math> to be the set of all <em id="Thmdefn4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Transformers</em>
<math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{TF}:\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}\to\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{TF}:\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}\to\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the form</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{TF}=\mathsf{TFBlock}^{(L)}\circ\mathsf{TFBlock}^{(L-1)}\circ\cdots\circ\mathsf{TFBlock}^{(1)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖳𝖥𝖡𝗅𝗈𝖼𝗄</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>𝖳𝖥𝖡𝗅𝗈𝖼𝗄</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>𝖳𝖥𝖡𝗅𝗈𝖼𝗄</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{TF}=\mathsf{TFBlock}^{(L)}\circ\mathsf{TFBlock}^{(L-1)}\circ\cdots\circ\mathsf{TFBlock}^{(1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefn4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{TFBlock}^{(\ell)}\equiv\mathsf{TFBlock}_{(\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}^{(\ell)},\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{(\ell)})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖳𝖥𝖡𝗅𝗈𝖼𝗄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>𝖳𝖥𝖡𝗅𝗈𝖼𝗄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>attn</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>ffn</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{TFBlock}^{(\ell)}\equiv\mathsf{TFBlock}_{(\theta_{\mathrm{attn}}^{(\ell)},\theta_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{(\ell)})}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Transformer block with <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> heads, model dimension <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}</annotation></semantics></math> and FFN width <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ffn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ffn}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmdefn4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[L]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For a Transformer <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{TF}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(L,H,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>TF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{TF}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(L,H,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})</annotation></semantics></math>, examples <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}=(x_{i},y_{i})_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}=(x_{i},y_{i})_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and a query <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we will slightly abuse our notation by writing <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{TF}(x,D_{n})\coloneqq\mathsf{TF}\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{in}}(D_{n},x)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{TF}(x,D_{n})\coloneqq\mathsf{TF}\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{in}}(D_{n},x)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> where the input matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathrm{in}}(D_{n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathrm{in}}(D_{n},x)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1 Transformers ‣ 3 Transformers, universal approximation and learnability ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Finally, we define <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Read}:\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Read}:\bigcup_{n=1}^{N}\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Read}(Z)\coloneqq Z_{n+1,d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Read}(Z)\coloneqq Z_{n+1,d+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathbb{R}^{(n+1)\times d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Read}\circ\mathsf{TF}(x,D_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Read}\circ\mathsf{TF}(x,D_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as the predicted value of the response at the query <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> based on examples <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Universal approximation and learnability</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now consider pretraining priors induced by randomly drawn regression functions, and prove universal approximation and learnability results for Transformers.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a measurable space of real-valued, measurable functions on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> that are uniformly bounded by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a random variable on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\perp\!\!\!\perp X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mpadded style="width:0.518em;" width="0.518em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\perp\!\!\!\perp X</annotation></semantics></math> be a zero-mean random variable on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the distribution of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{g}=g(X)+\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{g}=g(X)+\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. Further let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> be the distribution of the random measure <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\widetilde{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\widetilde{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the randomness comes from <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}\sim\widetilde{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}\sim\widetilde{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose throughout this subsection that there exist <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-finite measure <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and almost all realisations of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, the conditional distribution of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{g}\,|\,g(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo fence="false" lspace="0.448em">|</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{g}\,|\,g(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is absolutely continuous with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, with Radon–Nikodym derivative bounded by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, if the noise <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(0,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(0,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> distribution, then the conditional distribution of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m29" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y_{g}\,|\,\{g(X)=z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{g}\,|\,\{g(X)=z\}</annotation></semantics></math> has bounded Lebesgue density given by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m30" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y\mapsto\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^{2}}}e^{-(y-z)^{2}/(2\sigma^{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\mapsto\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^{2}}}e^{-(y-z)^{2}/(2\sigma^{2})}</annotation></semantics></math>. The following theorem shows that Transformers are universal approximators for the posterior regression function <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> given by (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that the activation function <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> in the FFN layers is Borel measurable and there is no polynomial <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=h</annotation></semantics></math> Lebesgue almost everywhere. Then for any <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}\geq d+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}\geq d+2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and a Transformer <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{TF}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(3,1,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>TF</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{TF}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(3,1,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{n\in[N]}\mathbb{E}_{P\sim\pi,(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)|P\sim P^{\otimes(n+1)}}\Bigl\{\bigl(\mathsf{Read}\circ\mathsf{TF}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})\bigr)^{2}\Bigr\}\leq\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{n\in[N]}\mathbb{E}_{P\sim\pi,(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)|P\sim P^{\otimes(n+1)}}\Bigl\{\bigl(\mathsf{Read}\circ\mathsf{TF}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})\bigr)^{2}\Bigr\}\leq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From now on, we will assume that the activation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathsf{ReLU},\mathsf{GELU},\mathsf{SiLU}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖫𝖴</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖦𝖤𝖫𝖴</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖲𝗂𝖫𝖴</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathsf{ReLU},\mathsf{GELU},\mathsf{SiLU}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are commonly used activation functions in modern Transformer models; see (<a href="#A1.E14" title="In Appendix A Notation for the appendix ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) for their definitions.
The following proposition shows that, when the noise <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> is sub-Gaussian, a large Transformer pretrained with sufficient data can achieve a <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-risk that is arbitrarily close to the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>-risk of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we do not require the input matrix or the parameters of the Transformer to be bounded. We achieve this by showing that the pseudo-dimension of the class is finite using results from a branch of mathematical logic called model theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that the noise <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> is sub-Gaussian. For any <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}\geq d+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}\geq d+2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following holds. Suppose <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}\geq d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}\geq d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\geq d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\geq d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) with <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\coloneqq\mathsf{Read}\circ\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(3,1,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>TF</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\coloneqq\mathsf{Read}\circ\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(3,1,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})</annotation></semantics></math> and pretraining data generated according to <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}\coloneqq\mathsf{clip}_{R}\circ\widehat{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖼𝗅𝗂𝗉</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}\coloneqq\mathsf{clip}_{R}\circ\widehat{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for all <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}(\widetilde{f}_{T})\coloneqq\mathbb{E}\mathcal{R}_{\pi}(\widetilde{f}_{T})-\mathcal{R}_{\pi}(g_{\pi})\leq\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}(\widetilde{f}_{T})\coloneqq\mathbb{E}\mathcal{R}_{\pi}(\widetilde{f}_{T})-\mathcal{R}_{\pi}(g_{\pi})\leq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This proposition confirms formally the intuition provided after Proposition <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> that the first term in the decomposition (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) can be made negligible.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Adaptivity and robustness of ICL</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we consider mixture distribution pretraining priors <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\lambda_{\alpha}\pi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\lambda_{\alpha}\pi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> for some finite index set <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, and test distribution <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi_{\beta})\leq\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi_{\beta})\leq\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that draws from <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> contain tasks of difficulty <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> with potential distribution shift relative to <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have already seen that Proposition <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides a decomposition of the ICL excess <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-risk, and Proposition <a href="#Thmthm3" title="Proposition 3. ‣ 3.2 Universal approximation and learnability ‣ 3 Transformers, universal approximation and learnability ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that the first term on the right-hand side of (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) can be made negligible. Our goal here, then, is to control the key quantity in the other term, namely
<math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math>, uniformly over all <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in the support of <math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. To this end, we specialise the general posterior contraction theory for nonparametric regression that we provide in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Bayesian nonparametric regression ‣ Appendix E Posterior contraction and adaptation ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a> to Besov regression functions with random smoothness (in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) and multi-index models (in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 In-context learning for multi-index models ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>In-context learning for Besov functions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the remainder of this section, we take<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            Our results in this section extend to any compact domain <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> by a scaling argument.</span></span></span> <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\coloneqq[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\coloneqq[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}\coloneqq\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>≔</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}\coloneqq\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since Besov functions can be characterised by their wavelet decomposition, we will consider regression functions with random wavelet coefficients. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}([0,1]^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}([0,1]^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of square integrable functions on <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and let</p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\bigl\{\Phi_{k}:k\in K\bigr\}\cup\bigl\{\Psi_{\ell,\gamma}:\ell\in[{\ell_{0}}:\infty),\,\gamma\in\Gamma_{\ell}\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bigl\{\Phi_{k}:k\in K\bigr\}\cup\bigl\{\Psi_{\ell,\gamma}:\ell\in[{\ell_{0}}:\infty),\,\gamma\in\Gamma_{\ell}\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">denote the tensor product Cohen–Daubechies–Vial (CDV) wavelet basis for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}([0,1]^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}([0,1]^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, constructed from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-regular and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-times continuously differentiable wavelets. The precise definitions of these wavelet functions are not important for us, and we will only use some basic properties of this wavelet basis, summarised in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Wavelets ‣ Appendix D Function spaces and wavelets ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,S)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> be the distribution of the random function</p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}\coloneqq\sum_{k\in K}C_{0}2^{-\ell_{0}d/2}a_{k}^{(\alpha)}\Phi_{k}+\sum_{\ell=\ell_{0}}^{\infty}\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma_{\ell}}C_{0}2^{-\ell(\alpha+d/2)}b_{\ell,\gamma}^{(\alpha)}\Psi_{\ell,\gamma}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}\coloneqq\sum_{k\in K}C_{0}2^{-\ell_{0}d/2}a_{k}^{(\alpha)}\Phi_{k}+\sum_{\ell=\ell_{0}}^{\infty}\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma_{\ell}}C_{0}2^{-\ell(\alpha+d/2)}b_{\ell,\gamma}^{(\alpha)}\Psi_{\ell,\gamma},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl(a_{k}^{(\alpha)},b_{\ell,\gamma}^{(\alpha)}:k\in K,\ell\in[\ell_{0}:\infty),\gamma\in\Gamma_{\ell}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(a_{k}^{(\alpha)},b_{\ell,\gamma}^{(\alpha)}:k\in K,\ell\in[\ell_{0}:\infty),\gamma\in\Gamma_{\ell}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> are independent random variables supported on <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with Lebesgue density bounded between <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}/2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}^{-1}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}^{-1}/2</annotation></semantics></math>. The scaling of the wavelet coefficients in (<a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) is chosen to ensure that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the Besov space <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\infty,\infty}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\infty,\infty}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> with smoothness <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, a function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\coloneqq\sum_{k\in K}a_{k}^{\prime}\Phi_{k}+\sum_{\ell=\ell_{0}}^{\infty}\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma_{\ell}}b_{\ell,\gamma}^{\prime}\Psi_{\ell,\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\coloneqq\sum_{k\in K}a_{k}^{\prime}\Phi_{k}+\sum_{\ell=\ell_{0}}^{\infty}\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma_{\ell}}b_{\ell,\gamma}^{\prime}\Psi_{\ell,\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, with deterministic wavelet coefficients, belongs to the Besov ball <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\infty,\infty}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d},C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\infty,\infty}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d},C)</annotation></semantics></math> of radius <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\ell_{0}d/2}\max_{k}|a_{k}^{\prime}|+\sup_{\ell,\gamma}2^{\ell(\alpha+d/2)}|b_{\ell,\gamma}^{\prime}|\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\ell_{0}d/2}\max_{k}|a_{k}^{\prime}|+\sup_{\ell,\gamma}2^{\ell(\alpha+d/2)}|b_{\ell,\gamma}^{\prime}|\leq C</annotation></semantics></math>; see Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Function spaces and wavelets ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. Thus, writing <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}\coloneqq\mathrm{supp}(\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}\coloneqq\mathrm{supp}(\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B_{\infty,\infty}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d},C_{0})\subseteq\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}\subseteq B_{\infty,\infty}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d},2C_{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B_{\infty,\infty}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d},C_{0})\subseteq\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}\subseteq B_{\infty,\infty}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d},2C_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">We remark that the Besov space <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\infty,\infty}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\infty,\infty}^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the Hölder space <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\notin\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\notin\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it contains <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\alpha}([0,1]^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>; see Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Function spaces ‣ Appendix D Function spaces and wavelets ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> for more details. Moreover, there exists <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\alpha,S,d}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\alpha,S,d}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}}\|g\|_{\infty}\leq C_{\alpha,S,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}}\|g\|_{\infty}\leq C_{\alpha,S,d}</annotation></semantics></math>; see (<a href="#A6.E54" title="In Proof. ‣ F.1 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix F Proofs for Section ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Borel probability distribution on <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with the property that there exists a hypercube <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}(A_{0})\geq c_{0}\mathrm{Vol}_{d}(A_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Vol</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}(A_{0})\geq c_{0}\mathrm{Vol}_{d}(A_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> for all measurable <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}\subseteq A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}\subseteq A</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\sim N(0,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\sim N(0,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> be independent. For a Borel measurable function <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:[0,1]^{d}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:[0,1]^{d}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the distribution of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{g}=g(X)+\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{g}=g(X)+\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. We then define <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the distribution of the random measure <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the randomness comes from the random regression function <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}\sim\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}\sim\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\alpha}\coloneqq\mathrm{supp}(\pi_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\alpha}\coloneqq\mathrm{supp}(\pi_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>. Letting <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\subseteq(0,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\subseteq(0,S)</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite set and letting <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{\alpha})_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{\alpha})_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of positive weights such that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\lambda_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\lambda_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, our pretraining prior <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be the mixture distribution</p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi\coloneqq\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\lambda_{\alpha}\pi_{\alpha}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi\coloneqq\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\lambda_{\alpha}\pi_{\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> consists of random regression tasks where the smoothness of the regression functions is randomly drawn from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> with probabilities given by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{\alpha})_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{\alpha})_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we define <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\coloneqq\bigcup_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\mathcal{P}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\coloneqq\bigcup_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\mathcal{P}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}\coloneqq\bigcup_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}\coloneqq\bigcup_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), the set <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a union of Besov balls with smoothness parameters in <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition provides the rate of convergence of the posterior regression, when the true regression belongs to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\circ}\in\mathcal{G}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\circ}\in\mathcal{G}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}\equiv P_{g^{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}\equiv P_{g^{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> be the prior distribution defined in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) and let <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the posterior regression function with respect to <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, not depending on <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)\sim P_{0}^{\otimes(n+1)}}\bigl[\bigl\{g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-g^{\circ}(X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]\leq Cn^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)\sim P_{0}^{\otimes(n+1)}}\bigl[\bigl\{g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-g^{\circ}(X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]\leq Cn^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Propositions <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#Thmthm3" title="Proposition 3. ‣ 3.2 Universal approximation and learnability ‣ 3 Transformers, universal approximation and learnability ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#Thmthm4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we are now ready to state the main theorem of this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exist <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}\geq d+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}\geq d+2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following holds. Suppose <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}\geq d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}\geq d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\geq d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\geq d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) with <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\coloneqq\mathsf{Read}\circ\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(3,1,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>TF</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\coloneqq\mathsf{Read}\circ\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(3,1,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})</annotation></semantics></math> and pretraining prior <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). Let <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\coloneqq\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\|g\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\coloneqq\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\|g\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}\coloneqq\mathsf{clip}_{R}\circ\widehat{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖼𝗅𝗂𝗉</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}\coloneqq\mathsf{clip}_{R}\circ\widehat{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for all <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large and <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sup_{\mu\,:\,\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi_{\beta})\leq\kappa}\mathcal{R}^{\mathrm{ICL}}_{\mu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})\leq Cn^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>ICL</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup_{\mu\,:\,\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi_{\beta})\leq\kappa}\mathcal{R}^{\mathrm{ICL}}_{\mu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})\leq Cn^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Transformer <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is pretrained on the mixture distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\lambda_{\alpha}\pi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal{A}}\lambda_{\alpha}\pi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> with different smoothness levels, whereas the test distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> consists of random regression functions of a fixed smoothness level <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, with a distribution shift such that <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi_{\beta})\leq\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi_{\beta})\leq\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>. When we evaluate the performance of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> under the test distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, Theorem <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that the ICL excess <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-risk of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is adaptive to the unknown smoothness <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, in the sense that its rate of convergence <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimal even if <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> were known; see Theorem <a href="#Thmthm8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 4.3 Lower bounds on the ICL excess risk ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Moreover, this risk bound holds uniformly over test distributions in a <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence ball around <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, although the Transformer is pretrained on tasks of different levels of difficulties, it is able to achieve faster (and optimal) rates of convergence on easier tasks (without knowing the difficulty of the tasks) and is robust to distribution shift.
Finally, we remark that <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ},d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ},d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> should increase with <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> in order to keep <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, for a Transformer with fixed architecture, fine tuning for downstream tasks may still be beneficial in cases where (i) the Transformer is not large enough, (ii) the amount of pretraining data is not sufficient, or (iii) there is significant distribution shift (<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is large).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>In-context learning for multi-index models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid the curse of dimensionality in the previous section, we will now consider multi-index models with<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            Again, our results in this section extend to any compact domain <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> by a scaling argument.</span></span></span> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}:\|x\|_{2}\leq 1\}\eqqcolon\mathbb{B}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>≕</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}:\|x\|_{2}\leq 1\}\eqqcolon\mathbb{B}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{p}(\mathbb{R}^{d})\coloneqq\bigl\{U\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times p}:U^{\top}U=I_{p}\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{p}(\mathbb{R}^{d})\coloneqq\bigl\{U\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times p}:U^{\top}U=I_{p}\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of all projection matrices from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> (also called the <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Stiefel manifold</em>). We will consider regression functions on <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of the form <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mapsto g\bigl((U^{\top}x+\bm{1}_{p})/2\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto g\bigl((U^{\top}x+\bm{1}_{p})/2\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in V_{p}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in V_{p}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> for some <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">effective dimension</em> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math> and Borel measurable <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:[0,1]^{p}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:[0,1]^{p}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we translate and scale <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\top}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\top}x</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure that it belongs to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,S)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}^{(p)}:[0,1]^{p}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}^{(p)}:[0,1]^{p}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be the random function (with smoothness <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) defined as in (<a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), but with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in the wavelet coefficients, and using the tensor product CDV wavelet basis for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}([0,1]^{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}([0,1]^{p})</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}([0,1]^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}([0,1]^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha,p}</annotation></semantics></math> be the distribution of the random function <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}_{\alpha,p}:\mathbb{B}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}_{\alpha,p}:\mathbb{B}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{g}_{\alpha,p}(x)\coloneqq\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}^{(p)}\biggl(\frac{(U^{(p)})^{\top}x+\bm{1}_{p}}{2}\biggr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{g}_{\alpha,p}(x)\coloneqq\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}^{(p)}\biggl(\frac{(U^{(p)})^{\top}x+\bm{1}_{p}}{2}\biggr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly distributed<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            One way to generate such a random matrix is to set <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U^{(p)}\coloneqq Z(Z^{\top}Z)^{-1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≔</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{(p)}\coloneqq Z(Z^{\top}Z)^{-1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="footnote5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times p</annotation></semantics></math> random matrix with independent <math id="footnote5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> entries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(e.g. <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chikuse, 2003</a>, Theorem 2.2.1(iii))</cite>.</span></span></span> on the Stiefel manifold <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{p}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{p}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, independently of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}_{\alpha}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\alpha,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\alpha,p}</annotation></semantics></math> for the support of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha,p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution on <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with the property that there exist <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a non-empty Euclidean ball <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq\mathbb{B}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq\mathbb{B}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}(A_{0})\geq c_{0}\mathrm{Vol}_{d}(A_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Vol</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}(A_{0})\geq c_{0}\mathrm{Vol}_{d}(A_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> for all measurable <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}\subseteq A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}\subseteq A</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then define <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\alpha,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\alpha,p}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the distribution of the random measure <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\widetilde{g}_{\alpha,p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\widetilde{g}_{\alpha,p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the randomness comes from the random regression function <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}_{\alpha,p}\sim\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}_{\alpha,p}\sim\widetilde{\pi}_{\alpha,p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\alpha,p}\coloneqq\mathrm{supp}(\pi_{\alpha,p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\alpha,p}\coloneqq\mathrm{supp}(\pi_{\alpha,p})</annotation></semantics></math>. For a finite set <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\prime}\subseteq(0,S)\times[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\prime}\subseteq(0,S)\times[d]</annotation></semantics></math> and a sequence of positive weights <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{\alpha,p})_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{\alpha,p})_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}\lambda_{\alpha,p}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}\lambda_{\alpha,p}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, our pretraining prior <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> for multi-index models is defined to be the mixture distribution</p>
<table id="S4.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi\coloneqq\sum_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}\lambda_{\alpha,p}\pi_{\alpha,p}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi\coloneqq\sum_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}\lambda_{\alpha,p}\pi_{\alpha,p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> generates random multi-index regression tasks, where the smoothness and effective dimensions of the regression functions are randomly drawn from <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with probabilities given by <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{\alpha,p})_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{\alpha,p})_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we define <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\coloneqq\bigcup_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}\mathcal{P}_{\alpha,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\coloneqq\bigcup_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}\mathcal{P}_{\alpha,p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}\coloneqq\bigcup_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}\mathcal{G}_{\alpha,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}\coloneqq\bigcup_{(\alpha,p)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}}\mathcal{G}_{\alpha,p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Proposition <a href="#Thmthm6" title="Proposition 6. ‣ 4.2 In-context learning for multi-index models ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is the analogue for multi-index models of Proposition <a href="#Thmthm4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6</span></span><span id="Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,r)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,r)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\circ}\in\mathcal{G}_{\beta,r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\circ}\in\mathcal{G}_{\beta,r}</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}\equiv P_{g^{\circ}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}\equiv P_{g^{\circ}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> be the prior distribution defined in (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.2 In-context learning for multi-index models ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) and let <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the posterior regression function with respect to <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, not depending on <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)\sim P_{0}^{\otimes(n+1)}}\bigl[\bigl\{g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-g^{\circ}(X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]\leq Cn^{-2\beta/(2\beta+r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{(\mathcal{D}_{n},X,Y)\sim P_{0}^{\otimes(n+1)}}\bigl[\bigl\{g_{\pi}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-g^{\circ}(X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]\leq Cn^{-2\beta/(2\beta+r)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Propositions <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#Thmthm3" title="Proposition 3. ‣ 3.2 Universal approximation and learnability ‣ 3 Transformers, universal approximation and learnability ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#Thmthm6" title="Proposition 6. ‣ 4.2 In-context learning for multi-index models ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we can now conclude that a pretrained Transformer is adaptive to the effective dimension of the multi-index models, as well as adapting to the smoothness of the regression function and being robust to distribution shift.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}\geq d+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}\geq d+2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following holds. Suppose <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{model}}\geq d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{model}}\geq d_{\mathrm{model}}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\geq d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ffn}}\geq d_{\mathrm{ffn}}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Problem set-up and ICL excess risk decomposition ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) with <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\coloneqq\mathsf{Read}\circ\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(3,1,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>TF</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ffn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\coloneqq\mathsf{Read}\circ\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{TF}}(3,1,d_{\mathrm{model}},d_{\mathrm{ffn}})</annotation></semantics></math> and pretraining prior <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.2 In-context learning for multi-index models ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). Let <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\coloneqq\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\|g\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\coloneqq\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\|g\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}\coloneqq\mathsf{clip}_{R}\circ\widehat{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖼𝗅𝗂𝗉</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}\coloneqq\mathsf{clip}_{R}\circ\widehat{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for all <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently large and <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,r)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,r)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sup_{\mu\,:\,\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi_{\beta,r})\leq\kappa}\mathcal{R}^{\mathrm{ICL}}_{\mu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})\leq Cn^{-2\beta/(2\beta+r)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>ICL</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup_{\mu\,:\,\chi^{2}(\mu,\pi_{\beta,r})\leq\kappa}\mathcal{R}^{\mathrm{ICL}}_{\mu}(\widetilde{f}_{T})\leq Cn^{-2\beta/(2\beta+r)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Lower bounds on the ICL excess risk</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our final theoretical contribution is to provide lower bounds on the ICL excess risk. Prior work has sought to establish the optimality of ICL algorithms via lower bounds on the minimax risk</p>
<table id="S4.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\inf_{\widehat{g}_{n}\in\widehat{\mathcal{G}}_{n}}\sup_{P\in\mathrm{supp}(\mu)}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{\widehat{g}_{n}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\inf_{\widehat{g}_{n}\in\widehat{\mathcal{G}}_{n}}\sup_{P\in\mathrm{supp}(\mu)}\mathbb{E}_{P}\bigl[\bigl\{\widehat{g}_{n}(X,\mathcal{D}_{n})-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y\,|\,X)\bigr\}^{2}\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{G}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{G}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of all measurable functions from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times(\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y})^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Although the minimax risk is often regarded as the gold standard in traditional statistical learning problems, the ICL excess <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-risk <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\mu}^{\mathrm{ICL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>ICL</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\mu}^{\mathrm{ICL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an expectation over <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\sim\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\sim\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, not a worst-case risk over <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathrm{supp}(\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathrm{supp}(\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus lower bounds on (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4.3 Lower bounds on the ICL excess risk ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) do not provide lower bounds for the ICL excess <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-risk. Theorem <a href="#Thmthm8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 4.3 Lower bounds on the ICL excess risk ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> below, however, does indeed provide a lower bound on the ICL excess <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-risk, and in a strong sense: even an estimator that has knowledge of the test distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> cannot achieve a better rate of convergence than the pretrained Transformers in Theorems <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#Thmthm7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 4.2 In-context learning for multi-index models ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8</span></span><span id="Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a) Under the setting of Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, let <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\ll\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\ll\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> be such that <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{P\in\mathcal{P}_{\beta}}\frac{\mathrm{d}\mu}{\mathrm{d}\pi_{\beta}}(P)&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{P\in\mathcal{P}_{\beta}}\frac{\mathrm{d}\mu}{\mathrm{d}\pi_{\beta}}(P)&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, not depending on <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\inf_{\widehat{g}_{n}\in\widehat{\mathcal{G}}_{n}}\mathcal{R}_{\mu}^{\mathrm{ICL}}(\widehat{g}_{n})\geq cn^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>ICL</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\inf_{\widehat{g}_{n}\in\widehat{\mathcal{G}}_{n}}\mathcal{R}_{\mu}^{\mathrm{ICL}}(\widehat{g}_{n})\geq cn^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b) In the setting of Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 In-context learning for multi-index models ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, let <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,r)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,r)\in\mathcal{A}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\ll\pi_{\beta,r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\ll\pi_{\beta,r}</annotation></semantics></math> be such that <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{P\in\mathcal{P}_{\beta,r}}\frac{\mathrm{d}\mu}{\mathrm{d}\pi_{\beta,r}}(P)&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{P\in\mathcal{P}_{\beta,r}}\frac{\mathrm{d}\mu}{\mathrm{d}\pi_{\beta,r}}(P)&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, not depending on <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\inf_{\widehat{g}_{n}\in\widehat{\mathcal{G}}_{n}}\mathcal{R}_{\mu}^{\mathrm{ICL}}(\widehat{g}_{n})\geq c^{\prime}n^{-2\beta/(2\beta+r)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>ICL</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\inf_{\widehat{g}_{n}\in\widehat{\mathcal{G}}_{n}}\mathcal{R}_{\mu}^{\mathrm{ICL}}(\widehat{g}_{n})\geq c^{\prime}n^{-2\beta/(2\beta+r)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We remark that, if <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as in Theorems <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 4.1 In-context learning for Besov functions ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> or <a href="#Thmthm7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 4.2 In-context learning for multi-index models ‣ 4 Adaptivity and robustness of ICL ‣ Provable test-time adaptivity and distributional robustness of in-context learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, then conditional on the pretraining data, we have <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{T}\in\widehat{\mathcal{G}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{T}\in\widehat{\mathcal{G}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgement</span>: The research of the first and third author was supported by RJS’s European Research Council Advanced Grant 101019498.</p>
</div>
</section>
